hi there steve kaufman  today i want to talk briefly about  grammar and about my recent trip to  peace river  um  i was recently up in northern alberta in  the peace river country which is  spectacular country  there are  great fields of wheat and canola which  is rapeseed  forest there's oil  not so many people but very friendly  people  and a rougher sort of different  lifestyle to what we have here in  vancouver  i fly to edmonton and then from edmonton  transfer you know take another flight to  grand prairie and then drive two and a  half hours north  um  the reason i mention it is because uh  while on my plane actually we were  flying from grand prairie to uh edmonton  there was a passenger who spoke only  french  and he was asking his neighbor who was a  lady who was this  basically an english speaker  what would it was a young fellow and  probably not a very experienced traveler  and he was trying to find out what  happens to his luggage  and so this lady was trying to explain  in her high school french she did quite  well compared to the level of most  uh english-speaking canadians  but what struck me was that here was  this woman who had quite a few words  but all her verbs were in the infinitive  you know  vu  whatever  and i have noticed that often  that  graduates of the canadian  uh you know english school system who  learned french  all they really remember is the words  the despite the emphasis on grammar and  in particular in french obviously in all  latin languages  the tenses are a problem because  depending on you know  uh person and number and  and yeah you know i you we  go did go you know went everything  changes and in english we're not used to  that so there's a lot of emphasis i  remember from my school days and i know  it hasn't changed a lot of emphasis on  the tenses and yet overwhelmingly people  graduate and they speak in the  infinitive  because  teaching people about grammar doesn't  enable them to produce it correctly  rather  lots of exposure does and that's why in  now granted english may be easier  insofar as tenses are concerned maybe  not  but uh swedish kids who watch a lot of  english language television speak much  better english  than  english speaking kids in canada who go  through french in school so being taught  grammar doesn't mean that you can  produce it correctly rather and i keep  on harping on this lots of exposure lots  of listening lots of reading eventually  speaking making mistakes picking up on  your mistakes listening some more  gradually that's going to ingrain these  habits because for a speaker of a  language which is not inflected very  much like english it's a difficult habit  to get into that even you you may know  theoretically you know je vette va ilvar  when you go to speak the brain doesn't  want to do it because it's not used to  doing it until you have had enough  exposure so i just wanted to make that  point and i'm going to it's going to be  a short one today and i'm going to end  up with a few  shots from my iphone  uh first you'll see some of the fields i  think these are canola fields a bumper  crop up there in the peace river and  then i went of course to visit our  sawmill up there and the mill manager  real and his wife kim  uh took us on the peace river and it's  fairly far north i mean there's not very  many people  and rather than uh normally we stay at  riya's place in the town of manning  which is about a thousand people  in the town  and lots of farms in the area the next  town is probably 100 you know  significant town is 100 kilometers away  um so anyway we went on the river up the  river a ways  to a little kind of cabin he has no  running water no electricity and we sat  there and we cooked up some steaks drank  some wine the wine is we had we got  extra protein from the flies that were  in our wine uh  and and and just again you know it was  like 30 degrees  celsius whatever that is in fahrenheit  during the day sunny and very warm  in the morning at around seven in the  morning as we were on the river heading  back to manning it was six degrees  celsius that's how big the difference is  so i will end up then with these videos  and thank you for listening bye for now  here we are as we're about to get on the  boat here  uh on the uh  mighty peace river  uh  delivered by that truck  and uh  about to get on  and i will uh shut this down before i  fall getting into the boat you can send  me yeah yeah so here we are we're  approaching this is an abandoned indian  lodge  this is where during the early wars  between the different tribes  this was the home of the chief  historical historical site  which and we're not allowed to trespass  here but the ours must do it regularly  and then we hope the rcmp doesn't find  us  we're gonna hit shore here all right  i thought you were gonna shave the like  little and then they don't have time  it's gonna get dark rub those sticks  together and all around last week  why don't you just scorch the meat  directly  anyway boy this is beautiful here  that is good  you know you would have liked romania

